examples.solution_text:
Plot the $y$-intercept $(0,1)$.

The gradient is $-\frac{2}{3}$. Move right $3$ and down $2$ to $(3,-1)$.

Rule a straight line through both points and extend it in both directions. The negative gradient makes the line slope down from left to right.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=6mm,y=6mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{lineblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{stepred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\draw[gridgrey,very thin,xstep=6mm,ystep=6mm] (-5,-5) grid (5,5);
\draw[black,<->] (-5.3,0)--(5.3,0);
\draw[black,<->] (0,-5.3)--(0,5.3);
\path (5.3,0) node[right] {$x$} (0,5.3) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \v in {-4,-2,2,4}{\draw[black] (\v,-0.08)--(\v,0.08);\draw[black] (-0.08,\v)--(0.08,\v);\path (\v,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};\path (0,\v) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\draw[lineblue,thick,<->] (-5,4.333333)--(5,-2.333333);
\draw[stepred,dashed,->] (0,1)--(3,1);
\draw[stepred,dashed,->] (3,1)--(3,-1);
\fill[black] (0,1) circle (1.6pt) (3,-1) circle (1.6pt);
\path (0,1) node[above left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$(0,1)$};
\path (3,-1) node[below right,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$(3,-1)$};
\path (1.5,1) node[above,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {right $3$};
\path (3,0.5) node[right,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {down $2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

There is no constant term, so $c=0$. Plot $(0,0)$, the origin.

The gradient is $\frac{3}{2}$. Move right $2$ and up $3$ to $(2,3)$.

Rule a straight line through both points and extend it in both directions.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=6mm,y=6mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{lineblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{stepred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\draw[gridgrey,very thin,xstep=6mm,ystep=6mm] (-5,-5) grid (5,5);
\draw[black,<->] (-5.3,0)--(5.3,0);
\draw[black,<->] (0,-5.3)--(0,5.3);
\path (5.3,0) node[right] {$x$} (0,5.3) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \v in {-4,-2,2,4}{\draw[black] (\v,-0.08)--(\v,0.08);\draw[black] (-0.08,\v)--(0.08,\v);\path (\v,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};\path (0,\v) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\draw[lineblue,thick,<->] (-3.333333,-5)--(3.333333,5);
\draw[stepred,dashed,->] (0,0)--(2,0);
\draw[stepred,dashed,->] (2,0)--(2,3);
\fill[black] (0,0) circle (1.6pt) (2,3) circle (1.6pt);
\path (0,0) node[below left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$(0,0)$};
\path (2,3) node[above left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$(2,3)$};
\path (1,0) node[below,yshift=-8mm,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {right $2$};
\path (2,1.5) node[right,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {up $3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Both lines have $c=-1$, so start at $(0,-1)$.

For $y=2x-1$, the gradient is $2=\frac{2}{1}$. Move right $1$ and up $2$ to $(1,1)$.

For $y=-x-1$, the gradient is $-1=\frac{-1}{1}$. Move right $1$ and down $1$ to $(1,-2)$.

Rule each line through its two points and extend it in both directions.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=6mm,y=6mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{lineblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{linered}{HTML}{EF6068}
\definecolor{gridgrey}{HTML}{CCCCCC}
\draw[gridgrey,very thin,xstep=6mm,ystep=6mm] (-5,-5) grid (5,5);
\draw[black,<->] (-5.3,0)--(5.3,0);
\draw[black,<->] (0,-5.3)--(0,5.3);
\path (5.3,0) node[right] {$x$} (0,5.3) node[above] {$y$};
\foreach \v in {-4,-2,2,4}{\draw[black] (\v,-0.08)--(\v,0.08);\draw[black] (-0.08,\v)--(0.08,\v);\path (\v,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};\path (0,\v) node[left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$\v$};}
\draw[lineblue,thick,<->] (-2,-5)--(3,5);
\draw[linered,thick,dashed,<->] (-5,4)--(4,-5);
\draw[black,dotted] (0,-1)--(1,-1)--(1,1);
\draw[black,dotted] (1,-1)--(1,-2);
\fill[black] (0,-1) circle (1.6pt) (1,1) circle (1.6pt) (1,-2) circle (1.6pt);
\path (0,-1) node[left,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$(0,-1)$};
\path (1,1) node[right,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$(1,1)$};
\path (1,-2) node[right,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$(1,-2)$};
\path (2.2,3.4) node[right,text=lineblue,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$y=2x-1$};
\path (-3,2) node[above,text=linered,fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$y=-x-1$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Both lines share the $y$-intercept $(0,-1)$. Their gradients differ: $y=2x-1$ rises from left to right, while $y=-x-1$ falls.
